592186045417] [17592186424805 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424649 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424805 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424688 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424805 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424805 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424757 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424805 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424805 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424805 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186424713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424805 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "efc3478e-663c-513f-98e8-1fbc5277d0d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424806 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424806 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424696 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424806 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424700 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424806 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424806 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186424749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424806 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424806 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424806 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186424749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424806 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f3caaf88-bd90-5d96-ae11-c1828ec82fce" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424807 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424779 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424807 #edb/ref 1069 "q-61586" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424807 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424807 #edb/ref 1071 "If a circle represents a whole, which model shows $210%?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424807 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "00b9b682-db5e-578e-b943-e60736c094da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424807 #edb/ref 1074 "First, recall that $210%$ is the same as $\\frac{210}{100}.$\n\nWe now need to simplify this fraction. Dividing the numerator and denominator by $10,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{210}{100}=\\frac{210÷10}{100÷10}=\\frac{21}{10}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we write $\\frac{21}{10}$ as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\frac{21}{10}=2\\,\\text{R}\\,1=2\\,\\frac{1}{10}\n$$\n\nThe model that shows $2\\,\\frac{1}{10}$ is:\n\n![](images/b9/b901819e40d67559b5b8f6d137ab5595531801f5529f778be34c768b2dc40c3f.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424808 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424785 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424808 #edb/ref 1069 "q-70168" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424808 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424808 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\frac{7}{10}\\times 6=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424808 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "028c8481-ba77-5958-9c80-addca2355a16" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424808 #edb/ref 1074 "To multiply a fraction by a whole number, we multiply the fraction's numerator by the whole number and keep the denominator the same:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7\\times 6}{10}=\\frac{42}{10}\n$$\n\nWe then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\frac{42}{10}=\\frac{42÷2}{10÷2}=\\frac{21}{5}\n$$\n\nFinally, we then convert $\\frac{21}{5}$ to a mixed number:\n\n$$\n21÷5=4\\,\\text{R}1=4\\,\\frac{1}{5}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424809 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424809 #edb/ref 1069 "q-92133" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424809 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424809 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b1/b1c4d24b853c33ae4ba367761eb207eacf7cc46312077e3f21416cfc05f5ef3b.png)\n\nThe model above represents $2$ wholes. Use this model to calculate the value of $2÷\\frac{1}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424809 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "02ac172b-9db9-5580-848c-c810f3c4dd7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424809 #edb/ref 1074 "To calculate $2÷\\frac{1}{3}$, we need to divide each of $2$ wholes into $3$ equal parts, as shown below.\n\n![](images/29/291ef5c96846fd1244b9be904da7d56db056befa0758b78c169f24b414d8b96e.png)\n\n \n\nWe get $2\\times 3=6$ pieces in total. \n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n2÷\\frac{1}{3}=6\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424810 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424790 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424810 #edb/ref 1069 "q-55068" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424810 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424810 #edb/ref 1071 "${53}^{4}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424810 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0cbd63dc-7d38-513f-8022-cfd6f02074bf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424810 #edb/ref 1074 "Here, ${53}^{4}$ is in exponent form. The base is $53$ and the exponent is $4.$ \n\nThis means that $53$ is multiplied by itself $4$ times:\n\n$$\n{{53\\times 53\\times 53\\times 53}_{⏟}}_{4\\text{ times}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{53}^{4}=53\\times 53\\times 53\\times 53.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424811 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424811 #edb/ref 1069 "q-138343" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424811 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424811 #edb/ref 1071 "$(7.2)^{2}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424811 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0faa0bbf-a97d-52f3-9471-5236871deb2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424811 #edb/ref 1074 "First, by writing the expression with multiplication signs, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(7.2)}^{2} & ={{7.2\\times 7.2}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ times}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe proceed by multiplying the two numbers just as we would with whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{1} \\\\ \\boxed{}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned} & \\,\\, & \\begin{aligned}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now count the number of decimal places in the $2$ factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in $7.2.$\nTherefore, the exponent $(7.2)^{2}$ will have $1\\times 2=2$ decimal places.\n\nSo, we take our value of $5184$ and add a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n51\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,{{84}_{⏟}}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $(7.2)^{2}=51.84.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424812 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424778 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424812 #edb/ref 1069 "q-136918" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424812 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424812 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\frac{32}{5}\\times \\frac{25}{8}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424812 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "152194d4-bfdf-57ac-b58d-963178280721" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424812 #edb/ref 1074 "Interpreting the fractions as division, we get\n\n$$\n(32\\,÷\\,5)\\times (25\\,÷\\,8).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can create an equivalent expression by swapping the $÷\\,5$ and $÷\\,8:$\n\n$$\n(32\\,÷\\,8)\\times (25\\,÷\\,5)\n$$\n\n \n\nInterpreting the divisions as fractions, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{32}{8}\\times \\frac{25}{5}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the divisions, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\frac{32}{8}}_{⏟}}_{4}\\times {{\\frac{25}{5}}_{⏟}}_{5}=4\\times 5=20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424813 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424787 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424813 #edb/ref 1069 "q-63721" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424813 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424813 #edb/ref 1071 "Determine the least common multiple of $4,$ $6$ and $8.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424813 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "19af72ca-8e82-500b-a6d5-3bf3c4fb4f5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424813 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's list the multiples of each number. Then, we find the smallest number that appears in all three lists:\n\n- Multiples of $4$: $\\,4,8,12,16,20,24,28…$\n\n- Multiples of $6$: $\\,6,12,18,24,30…$\n\n- Multiples of $8$: $\\,8,16,24,32…$\n\nWe conclude that the least common multiple of  $4,$ $6$ and $8$ is $24.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424814 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424814 #edb/ref 1069 "q-150960" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424814 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424814 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d1/d1e17f92f13f6929a55967bd2fcf9f963adf074056affcedcdd753ed6f68aec7.png)\n\nWhat is the correct way to represent the line segment shown above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424814 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2765417b-abb7-5b0c-b51e-af1cad40b562" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424814 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the segment has endpoints $S$ and $T,$ we can represent this line segment as follows:\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{ST}\n$$\n\nWe draw a horizontal line $(\\overset{―}{})$ with no arrows above our letters to denote that the line does not extend forever." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424815 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424800 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424815 #edb/ref 1069 "q-43595" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424815 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424815 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following points lies  in the fourth quadrant?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424815 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2919c042-14cc-564a-b488-e11edad346eb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424815 #edb/ref 1074 "- The point $(2,-2)$ is below and to the right of the origin, so it is in the fourth quadrant $✓$\n\n- The point $(1,1)$ is above and to the right of the origin, so it is in the first quadrant $\\times $\n\n- The point $(2,3)$ is above and to the right of the origin, so it is in the first quadrant $\\times $\n\n- The point $(-1,-1)$ is below and to the left of the origin, so it is in the third quadrant $\\times $\n\n- The point $(-2,2)$ is above and to the left of the origin, so it is in the second quadrant $\\times $" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424816 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424816 #edb/ref 1069 "q-43156" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424816 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424816 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is undefined?\n\nI. $0\\cdot 0$\n\nII. $\\frac{2}{0}$\n\nIII. $\\frac{0}{-1}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424816 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3913baef-cd96-5f27-bb8b-fbabac946e82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424816 #edb/ref 1074 "We consider each point individually:\n\n- Multiplying any number by zero always gives zero. Therefore, $0\\cdot 0=0.$\n\n- We cannot divide any number by zero. Therefore, $\\frac{2}{0}=2÷0$ is undefined.\n\n- Zero divided by any number (other than zero) equals zero. Therefore, $\\frac{0}{-1}=0÷(-1)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424817 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424817 #edb/ref 1069 "q-67037" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424817 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424817 #edb/ref 1071 "Translate the following phrase into an algebraic expression: \"$2$ less than a number.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424817 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3e293dd9-81d5-5834-9d60-552159e9c226" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424817 #edb/ref 1074 "If we denote our number by $b$, then the phrase can be written as\n\n$$\nb-2.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424818 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424801 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424818 #edb/ref 1069 "q-57929" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424818 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424818 #edb/ref 1071 "$6\\,\\frac{7}{8}-1\\,\\frac{5}{6}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424818 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4123159f-01d7-597f-9386-b9f6aae3c918" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424818 #edb/ref 1074 "To subtract two mixed numbers, we subtract whole numbers from whole numbers and fractions from fractions. \n\nSubtracting the whole numbers, we get $6-1=5.$\n\nNow we subtract the fractions:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{8}-\\frac{5}{6}\n$$\n\nTo subtract the fractions, we need to find the least common multiple of the denominators ($8$ and $6$).\n\n- Multiples of 8: $\\,8,16,24,…$\n\n- Multiples of 6: $\\,6,12,18,24,…$\n\nTherefore, the least common denominator of $8$ and $6$ is $24.$\n\n- To put $\\frac{7}{8}$ over a denominator of $24,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{8}=\\frac{7\\times 3}{8\\times 3}=\\frac{21}{24}\n$$\n\n- To put $\\frac{5}{6}$ over a denominator of $24,$ we multiply the numerator and denominator by $4$:\n\n$$\n\\frac{5}{6}=\\frac{5\\times 4}{6\\times 4}=\\frac{20}{24}\n$$\n\nWe can now subtract the fractions. We keep the denominator the same, and we subtract the numerators:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{7}{8}-\\frac{5}{6}=\\frac{21}{24}-\\frac{20}{24}=\\frac{1}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n6\\,\\frac{7}{8}-1\\,\\frac{5}{6}=5\\,\\frac{1}{24}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424819 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424784 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424819 #edb/ref 1069 "q-41363" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424819 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424819 #edb/ref 1071 "${(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{3}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424819 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4dd6b555-5ecc-595c-aed6-3fa1e6dff4c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424819 #edb/ref 1074 "To work out ${(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{3},$ we multiply $-\\frac{2}{3}$ by itself and then multiply the result by $-\\frac{2}{3}$ again:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(-\\frac{2}{3})}^{3} & =(-\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{3})\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\cdot 2\\cdot 2}{3\\cdot 3\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{27}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424820 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424782 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424820 #edb/ref 1069 "q-108513" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424820 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424820 #edb/ref 1071 "Translate the following phrase into an algebraic expression: \"The quotient of a number and two less than that number.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424820 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5d05e800-2322-572d-b914-4605eb307837" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424820 #edb/ref 1074 "If we denote our number by $x$, then \"two less than that number\" can be written as\n\n$$\nx-2.\n$$\n\nSo, the whole phrase can be written as \n\n$$\n\\frac{x}{x-2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424821 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424802 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424821 #edb/ref 1069 "q-52571" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424821 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424821 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f1/f1c2ae86cd726df7a493351ad0cdb29fdb9767db75c25b432c1832f97865e1dc.png)\n\nFrom the diagram above, which of the following statements is true?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424821 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6d3b19cb-cf63-59ba-9591-d8e9da347a6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424821 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose $a$ and $b$ are two lines. Then,\n\n- $a∥b$ means $a$ and $b$ are parallel, and\n\n- $a⊥b$ means $a$ and $b$ are perpendicular.\n\nIn the given diagram, there are two pairs of perpendicular lines:\n\n- $q$ is perpendicular to $r,$ which we write as $q⊥r$\n\n- $q$ is perpendicular to $s,$ which we write as $q⊥s.$\n\n![](images/06/063ecc105da75d9ba1b3c242476f1353dec82ca9ec13df48f4f480f5f490cb94.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct statement is $q⊥r$ and $q⊥s.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424822 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424797 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424822 #edb/ref 1069 "q-136486" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424822 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424822 #edb/ref 1071 "$∣-9.2\\,∣=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424822 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7e488715-4e78-55fa-9093-dc4d45898e40" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424822 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the absolute value of $-9.2,$ we simply drop the negative sign:\n\n$$\n∣-9.2\\,∣=9.2\n$$\n\nThe absolute value of a number gives the distance between the number and zero on a number line.\n\n![](images/73/73e7a7cda9a920826aa6314b99689525de7c8dadc2a937adb87f5b4fd5042e4b.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424823 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424789 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424823 #edb/ref 1069 "q-92949" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424823 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424823 #edb/ref 1071 "What is missing in the following sentence?\n\nIn a music school, the ratio of students in the violin class to students in the piano class is $32:4,$ so there are $\\boxed{}$ students in the violin class for each student in the piano class." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424823 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7fb16d63-026a-5791-8719-a48e4a4e389e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424823 #edb/ref 1074 "We can rewrite the ratio $32:4$ as the following fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{32}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $4:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{32}{4}=\\frac{32÷4}{4÷4}=\\frac{8}{1}=8\n$$\n\nThis means that there are $8$ students in the violin class for each student in the piano class." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424824 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424777 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424824 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99463" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424824 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424824 #edb/ref 1071 "$(-5)\\cdot (3.3)=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424824 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8d1edec4-291f-530b-ae4b-c5263dbe2c85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424824 #edb/ref 1074 "We have a product of a negative number $(-5)$ and a positive number $(3.3).$ So the product will be negative:\n\n$$\n(-5)\\cdot 3.3=-\\boxed{}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the number that goes in the box, we multiply $-5$ by $3.3$ without any signs: $5\\cdot 3.3=16.5.$ So, we get\n\n$$\n(-5)\\cdot 3.3=-16.5.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424825 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424825 #edb/ref 1069 "q-99858" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424825 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424825 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the reciprocal of $3\\,\\frac{2}{5}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424825 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "905949ae-4813-5860-b334-182f5d44f611" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424825 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we convert the given mixed number to an improper fraction:\n\n$$\n3\\,\\frac{2}{5}=\\frac{(3\\times 5)+2}{5}=\\frac{17}{5}\n$$\n\nTo find the reciprocal of a fraction, we switch the numerator and the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{17}{5}\\,\\to \\,\\frac{5}{17}\n$$\n\nSo the reciprocal of $\\frac{17}{5}$ is $\\frac{5}{17}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424826 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424788 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424826 #edb/ref 1069 "q-136916" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424826 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424826 #edb/ref 1071 "$\\frac{15}{8}\\times \\frac{24}{5}=$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424826 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b89690ae-8c80-573d-99a2-301f29ba824c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424826 #edb/ref 1074 "Interpreting the fractions as division, we get\n\n$$\n(15\\,÷\\,8)\\times (24\\,÷\\,5).\n$$\n\n \n\nSince our expression contains multiplication and division only, we can create an equivalent expression by swapping the $÷\\,8$ and $÷\\,5:$\n\n$$\n(15\\,÷\\,5)\\times (24\\,÷\\,8)\n$$\n\n \n\nInterpreting the divisions as fractions, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{15}{5}\\times \\frac{24}{8}.\n$$\n\nCarrying out the divisions, we arrive at\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{\\frac{15}{5}}_{⏟}}_{3}\\times {{\\frac{24}{8}}_{⏟}}_{3}=3\\times 3=9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424827 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424795 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424827 #edb/ref 1069 "q-71254" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424827 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424827 #edb/ref 1071 "Use a visual fraction model to find the value of $3+\\frac{2}{7}$ as an improper fraction." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424827 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bee69b89-dd1d-5978-a866-55d032eb0cd5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424827 #edb/ref 1074 "Writing $3+\\frac{2}{7}$ as a visual fraction model, we get the following:\n\n![](images/97/9797ccbc74bee1628f6c056415b831ee315d1e5d5b6c01496abdcc7b62537b2b.png)\n\nThe shape on the right is split into $7$ equal parts. We can split the first, second, and third shapes into $7$ equal parts as follows:\n\n![](images/3b/3b8c180326206b70caf96caef6d560ac57621a61a5bd3fc85d9e3bb1e7dfebf8.png)\n\nThere are $7+7+7+2=23$ shaded parts in total. Therefore, their sum is $\\frac{23}{7}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424828 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186424806 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424828 #edb/ref 1069 "q-60095" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424828 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424828 #edb/ref 1071 "The ratio of vanilla flavored cakes to total cakes in a bakery is $5:9.$ What is the meaning of this ratio?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424828 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c904b7d0-fa86-585f-90ce-acc0a098264d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186424828 #edb/ref 1074 "If the ratio of vanilla flavored cakes to total cakes is $5:9,$ it means that there are $5$ vanilla flavored cakes for every $9$ cakes in total.\n\nAnother way of writing this is to say that \"out of every $9$ cakes in the bakery, $5$ of them are vanilla flavored cakes.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
